{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788217868,"nonce":"wtyzTVdj/b6C5VMptKGCXAo9TJA=","title":"A gate-refused order writes no action row, so half of 4087644's journal rule is unimplemented","message":"Found by running `accept/4087644-order-honesty.py` on the bench, session 9,\nafter 4087644 merged. 3 failures of 51; the shape contract passed clean, so\nthese are behaviour, not envelope paths.\n\n## 5.1b — the important one\n\nAn undrafted doctor sent `tend` is correctly refused by the drafted-only gate\n(5.1a passes) and writes **`journal_seq: 0`** — no action row.\n\nBut `already-doing-it` DOES write one (1.3a passes). So the journal rule landed\nfor one gate and not for rejections generally.\n\nThat matters because the rule was the point. 4087644's comment #1 argues that\nthe wasted orders are exactly the ones invisible to the journal, and the journal\nis the aggregate the agent learns from — so \"which of my instructions are\nredundant\" stays unanswerable for every gate that still writes nothing. The\norchestrator ruled comment #1 supersedes the body's \"writes no action row\"\nduring this round's vet; the merged code implements that ruling for\n`already-doing-it` only.\n\n**Consequence already in the tree:** `accept/3.4-pawn-orders.py` check 5.12c was\nchanged to `journal_seq \u003e= 1` in `7f1a825` on the strength of that ruling. It\nnow asserts the intended contract, which the code does not meet, so 3.4 will\nfail there until this is fixed. That is the correct state — the assertion is\nright and the code is behind it — but it must not be \"fixed\" by reverting the\nassertion.\n\n## 2.2b — the `ordered` triple does not generalise\n\n`data.state.ordered` reads FALSE on a job we ordered, while `job_giver` is\ncorrectly `ThinkNode_QueuedJob` (2.2a passes) and `player_forced` is true (2.3\npasses).\n\nThe triple is `jobGiver is ThinkNode_QueuedJob \u0026\u0026 workGiverDef != null \u0026\u0026\nplayerForced`. It came from 4087644's comment #1, where it was derived to\nseparate an ordered job from `JobGiver_Work.TryIssueJobPackage`'s autonomous\n`playerForced = true` on the emergency-prioritized branch. That derivation is\nsound for WORK-GIVER jobs. It does not hold for a direct order like `wear`,\nwhich has no `workGiverDef` at all — so `ordered` is false for exactly the\norders most obviously ordered.\n\nNeeds a discriminator that is correct for both shapes, or `ordered` must be\ndocumented as meaning \"ordered work\" specifically. Do not simply drop the\n`workGiverDef` clause without re-checking the JobGiver_Work case it exists to\nexclude.\n\n## 3.2a — a queued order does not appear in the queue\n\n`data.state.job_queue.total` is 0 after a `queue:true` order, and the suite's\nown note reads \"queue total moved but no rows came back\", which is\nself-contradictory and needs its own look. 3.3 then could not produce a\ncollision on the queued path, so that gate went unexercised this run.\n\nLeast diagnosed of the three. May be a publication bug, may be that the queued\njob completed before the read.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A gate-refused order writes an action row carrying its verdict, for EVERY\n  gate, not just `already-doing-it`. `accept/3.4-pawn-orders.py` 5.12c passes\n  unmodified.\n- `data.state.ordered` is true for a direct order such as `wear`, or the field\n  is renamed/documented to mean ordered WORK and the suite asserts that instead.\n- `job_queue.total` reflects a queued order, and 3.3's collision gate is\n  actually exercised rather than skipped.\n- `accept/4087644-order-honesty.py` reaches 51 of 51 with no red errors.","files":null}]}